Agents work in parallel. The protected branch does not. Every pull request
that wants to land queues up, and a conventional merge queue answers each turn
the same way: build the candidate, run a fresh cloud CI job, merge, repeat.
Your agents already did the work. The cloud sets aside that passing local run
on the branch and replays it anyway.

Thwip Queue keeps the safety of a queue and drops the replay. It merges on the
evidence that already exists: the signed proof your agent attached to the
branch. The queue pins the exact candidate, offers the lane to the first pull
request whose proof still holds, and records every move as a signed
transition. Nothing reruns unless your policy says it should.

## The same lane, a deliberate work loop

The queue still serializes the one operation that must be serial: writing the
protected branch. One lane per branch, one eligible pull request at a time,
one writer, the Thwip GitHub App. What changes is what happens between turns.

When a pull request is ready, the queue builds the pinned candidate: the exact
merge GitHub has prepared from the current base and the current PR head. Then
the policy decides where the evidence for that candidate comes from:

| Profile | What happens | Trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fast` | Reuse the signed proof already attached to the PR head. | Fastest; trusts the key that signed it. |
| `fresh` | A project agent re-runs the pinned candidate and signs a receipt. | No cloud rerun; needs a project runner. |
| `remote` | A pinned GitHub Actions verifier runs the candidate. | Conventional CI reference; one run per candidate. |
| `audited` | The agent run, then a GitHub Actions rerun of the same suites. | Strongest; pays both costs. |

The profiles are a progression, and the right one is a per-project choice
about where the rerun is worth the minutes. `fast` is the one the queue is
built around: the branch already carries a proof of the exact tree, so the
turn costs nothing to re-verify. `thwip / turn` is the check that says whose turn it is, and it goes
green the moment the queue offers the lane. Waiting pull requests sit *in
progress* rather than failing. Waiting is not a failure.

## What a turn looks like

The queue evaluates the exact pull-request head, offers the lane, and merges
with a merge commit whose first parent is the base it verified. Each movement
writes a signed record: which candidate, which base, which policy version,
which evidence. Anyone can fetch the record bytes and verify the branch chain
without a Thwip account.

## Getting started

Machine setup is one agent prompt ([install](/install)); repository setup is
one guided path in the [dashboard](/dashboard) that opens the queue pull
request with the required workflows. From a branch that carries a valid proof:

```bash
thwip ship --wait
```

The queue does the rest, and writes the record that says how it did it.

